<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hidalgo (state)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hidalgo is a state of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a>. It contains the archaeological site of Tula, capital of the Toltecs in the Early Postclassic period, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of Pachuca and Real del Monte, worked since the colonial period. The state was created in 1869 and is named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>, leader of the independence rising of 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hidalgo-state.jpg" alt="Hidalgo (state)" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CanyonSouthPe%C3%B1aHuasca.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
